          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Thomas Mann: Early Life</strong><br>\nThomas Mann was born on June 6, 1875, in Lübeck, Germany, into a wealthy merchant family. He was the second son of Thomas Johann Heinrich Mann and Júlia da Silva Bruhns. Mann attended a science secondary school in Lübeck and later moved to Munich with his family where he spent most of his life. His early experiences in the merchant class and his family's later financial difficulties influenced much of his writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginnings of a Writer</strong><br>\nMann began his writing career as a journalist, working for the magazine \"Simplicissimus\" after dropping out of university. His first book, \"Little Mr. Friedemann,\" was published in 1898. These early works were characterized by a deep analysis of the bourgeois culture he was raised in, using a style marked by irony and social criticism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Buddenbrooks: Breakthrough Novel</strong><br>\nMann's first great success was the novel \"Buddenbrooks,\" published in 1901. This book, which chronicled the decline of a wealthy merchant family in Lübeck over four generations, reflected Mann's own family experiences. The novel was praised for its rich detail and understanding of the human condition, leading to Mann's reputation as a significant literary figure.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
