          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Exploring the Artist's Role</strong><br>\nIn subsequent works, Mann explored the role and psyche of the artist in society. \"Death in Venice,\" published in 1912, is one of his most famous novellas, which delves into the obsession and decadence of an aging writer. This novella and others show Mann's fascination with the conflicting drives of creativity and civilized restraint.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Views and Exile</strong><br>\nMann's political views evolved over time, and he became a critic of the Nazi regime. When Hitler came to power in 1933, Mann was on vacation in Switzerland and never returned to Germany. His outspoken criticisms of the Nazis led to the revocation of his German citizenship in 1936, and he spent the rest of his life in exile.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1929, Mann was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature, primarily in recognition of \"Buddenbrooks,\" which the Nobel Committee described as \"one of the classic works of contemporary literature.\" The prize cemented his status as one of the leading figures in world literature.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
