          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Works and Themes</strong><br>\nMann continued to write prolifically through his exile years. His later works, like \"Joseph and His Brothers,\" were marked by philosophical and theological depth, exploring themes of fate, history, and mythology. These works were known for their complexity and academic nature, catering to a more intellectual audience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Family Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nMann was married to Katia Pringsheim, with whom he had six children. His children Erika and Klaus also became famous writers. Mann's legacy is complex; he was a family man, a representative of the German literary scene, and an exile with a strong sense of social responsibility. His work continues to be studied for its literary merit and historical insights.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Remembrance</strong><br>\nThomas Mann died on August 12, 1955, in Zurich, Switzerland. His works have left an indelible mark on literature, with his exploration of social, political, and human themes. Mann is remembered not only for his writing but also for his role as a cultural commentator and critic during tumultuous times in history.</p>\n"
              },
